1
00:00:01,174 --> 00:00:04,337
[SPEAKER_04]: So, this is an excerpt from Majjhima Nikaya 41.

2
00:00:08,061 --> 00:00:12,886
[SPEAKER_04]: Householders, there are three kinds of bodily conduct not in accordance with the dhamma.

3
00:00:13,547 --> 00:00:17,731
[SPEAKER_04]: Unrighteous conduct, there are four kinds of verbal conduct not in accordance with the dhamma.

4
00:00:18,852 --> 00:00:22,636
[SPEAKER_04]: And there are three kinds of mental conduct not in accordance with the dhamma.

5
00:00:25,425 --> 00:00:44,900
[SPEAKER_04]: It's when somebody kills living beings, when somebody takes by way of theft the wealth and property of others, when he misconducts himself in sensual pleasures,

6
00:00:46,318 --> 00:00:51,041
[SPEAKER_04]: Meaning, he has improper sexual relationships.

7
00:00:52,923 --> 00:00:57,265
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, there are four kinds of verbal conduct, not in the course of the dhamma.

8
00:00:58,286 --> 00:01:14,297
[SPEAKER_04]: When somebody lies, when they... when they speak maliciously, so as to divide groups, divide one group from the other group, or divide the other group from the first group.

9
00:01:16,011 --> 00:01:28,757
[SPEAKER_04]: When one speaks harshly, and then when they speak basically useless talk, or frivolous talk.

10
00:01:29,377 --> 00:01:33,459
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, there are three kinds of mental conduct, not in accordance with the dhamma.

11
00:01:34,279 --> 00:01:35,540
[SPEAKER_04]: The first one is covetousness.

12
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:37,921
[SPEAKER_04]: The second one is ill will.

13
00:01:39,503 --> 00:01:41,006
[SPEAKER_04]: And the third one is wrong view.

14
00:01:42,168 --> 00:01:44,612
[SPEAKER_04]: And here is the part of interest.

15
00:01:44,692 --> 00:01:45,032
[SPEAKER_04]: It says,

16
00:01:50,113 --> 00:02:13,470
[SPEAKER_04]: There is nothing given, nothing offered, nothing sacrificed, no fruit or result of good and bad actions, no this world, no other world, no mother, no father, no beings who were reborn spontaneously, no good and virtuous recluses and brahmins in the world who have themselves realized by direct knowledge and the claim of this world and the other world.

17
00:02:14,270 --> 00:02:16,572
[SPEAKER_04]: Those are the three types of mental misconduct.

18
00:02:18,317 --> 00:02:33,468
[SPEAKER_04]: So, householders, it is by reason of such conduct, not in accordance with the dhamma, that some beings here, on the dissolution of the body after death, reappear in states of deprivation, an unhappy destination, a perdition, even in hell.

19
00:02:34,849 --> 00:02:45,097
[SPEAKER_04]: So, basically, the... it's something that people might overlook, that

20
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:58,657
[SPEAKER_00]: The wrong view is on the same level as these actions that a person might have overcome and, you know, just through basic virtue and keeping the five precepts, but...

21
00:03:01,675 --> 00:03:07,957
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds like a very coarse or crude kind of view.

22
00:03:08,577 --> 00:03:09,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody actually believes that.

23
00:03:10,818 --> 00:03:17,780
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds as if it's something that everybody would have gone beyond just by becoming a Buddhist, for example.

24
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:29,263
[SPEAKER_00]: But that wrong view is actually something that is much more deeply rooted and much more

25
00:03:31,165 --> 00:03:34,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, if you fully abandon that view, you would be a sotāpanna.

26
00:03:35,146 --> 00:03:48,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there have... there has to be traces of that view in your experience, even if you don't outright tell others like, no, I believe that there is nothing given, or I believe that there is no mother and no father.

27
00:03:49,211 --> 00:03:54,673
[SPEAKER_00]: There is some trace of that in you, if you are not a sotāpanna.

28
00:03:54,713 --> 00:03:56,714
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, that...

29
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:10,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I thought we could explore how would those views manifest, given that it's not the obvious intention to believe these things.

30
00:04:33,007 --> 00:04:40,631
[SPEAKER_01]: So, here we start with the beginning, like there's nothing given, nothing offered, nothing sacrificed.

31
00:04:44,053 --> 00:04:45,134
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I set a wrong view.

32
00:04:50,177 --> 00:04:51,878
[SPEAKER_04]: And what does that mean exactly?

33
00:04:51,938 --> 00:04:59,702
[SPEAKER_02]: Does it mean to say... Is it talking about... like...

34
00:05:04,235 --> 00:05:12,799
[SPEAKER_00]: What it's really saying is it makes no difference whether you give something to someone or not.

35
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:15,100
[SPEAKER_00]: It's connected with the second point.

36
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:16,941
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no result of good and bad actions.

37
00:05:17,001 --> 00:05:19,102
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, it just makes no difference.

38
00:05:19,142 --> 00:05:23,984
[SPEAKER_00]: You just go through life and it's kind of like random.

39
00:05:24,064 --> 00:05:28,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that happen to you are a result of just

40
00:05:29,807 --> 00:05:34,329
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, random things happening, the world, chaos theory.

41
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:36,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Some cosmic force or other.

42
00:05:36,330 --> 00:05:36,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

43
00:05:37,691 --> 00:05:52,297
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it basically... the reason why that view is incompatible with the right view is because, I mean, the most basic point is it just takes away the fact that you are responsible for your own suffering, most important.

44
00:05:53,257 --> 00:05:58,760
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can't accept, it's not just like the fact that you won't be making merit.

45
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:05,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you have that wrong view, it's the fact that it blocks the understanding of the dhamma itself.

46
00:06:05,606 --> 00:06:18,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then you... like, how are you going to recognize that you, yourself... Because results of good and bad action doesn't just mean bodily and verbal action.

47
00:06:18,298 --> 00:06:21,160
[SPEAKER_00]: As this itself said, it talks about mental action as well.

48
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:23,322
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how will you purify yourself?

49
00:06:24,323 --> 00:06:31,287
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't believe that the actions that you're doing have results, it's just the most basic thing.

50
00:06:31,647 --> 00:06:31,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

51
00:06:32,688 --> 00:06:35,270
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that first one is always enough.

52
00:06:35,350 --> 00:06:41,953
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, what does it mean, for example, not to believe that there is a mother and father?

53
00:06:48,637 --> 00:06:49,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, most generally,

54
00:06:50,683 --> 00:06:53,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Things in the world come with their own significance.

55
00:06:55,204 --> 00:07:07,007
[SPEAKER_01]: And in terms of one's relationship with human beings, one's father and mother are... they have a special significance.

56
00:07:08,987 --> 00:07:10,648
[SPEAKER_01]: And so it is important to

57
00:07:11,548 --> 00:07:14,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognize, on the most general level, things have significance.

58
00:07:15,810 --> 00:07:31,717
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if there are unpleasant aspects connected with that, to not deny the significance and just see everything as basically equal, like there is no father, there is no mother, and just say, well, it's... they're just all human beings.

59
00:07:32,317 --> 00:07:33,917
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can extend that principle further.

60
00:07:34,498 --> 00:07:34,798
[SPEAKER_01]: And then,

61
00:07:35,818 --> 00:07:39,582
[SPEAKER_01]: In the wrong way, start taking things apart, as if it almost doesn't exist.

62
00:07:40,242 --> 00:07:44,026
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be a sort of continuation of that same wrong view.

63
00:07:44,746 --> 00:07:56,756
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is that, as an infant, you're fully dependent on, let's say, parents or caretakers generally, and without their support, you simply wouldn't have made it.

64
00:07:57,775 --> 00:08:04,725
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's another discourse where the Buddha, in fact, indicates that it's not easy to repay what one's parents have done.

65
00:08:05,507 --> 00:08:08,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And there are some telling similes there as well.

66
00:08:09,773 --> 00:08:12,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to to see that...

67
00:08:18,113 --> 00:08:24,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then the question is, yeah, do you have a say in the significance that things have?

68
00:08:24,440 --> 00:08:27,443
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, that's a good question to ask in this context.

69
00:08:27,923 --> 00:08:35,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, am I creating the significance of things that I encounter in my world, or is that something that I sort of find myself in?

70
00:08:36,985 --> 00:08:37,205
[SPEAKER_02]: What?

71
00:08:37,866 --> 00:08:42,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because it's not that people want... it's not that...

72
00:08:45,249 --> 00:08:50,591
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem, basically, is that you would start denying, naturally, when you try to overcome suffering.

73
00:08:51,051 --> 00:09:04,477
[SPEAKER_00]: The natural direction is, again, as you said, to start denying the significance of things, because the significance of things is misunderstood to be suffering, or is misunderstood to be greed, aversion, and delusion.

74
00:09:04,497 --> 00:09:05,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example,

75
00:09:05,938 --> 00:09:19,887
[SPEAKER_00]: You would confuse the fact that there is a special significance to your parents, as you said, because they have raised you and they have, like, it just doesn't matter what you do, for better or worse.

76
00:09:20,187 --> 00:09:24,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you hate your parents, it's always going to have some sort of special significance.

77
00:09:24,951 --> 00:09:29,794
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, if you then start thinking that, you know, being free from craving, for example,

78
00:09:30,454 --> 00:09:43,460
[SPEAKER_00]: means not to... that, you know, your parents don't matter anymore because you have no attachment, you have no... you have no concern for the world, you've abandoned all concern for the world.

79
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:49,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you would be implicitly assuming, like you would be overshooting the mark,

80
00:09:50,043 --> 00:09:54,504
[SPEAKER_00]: And seeing, oh, so I have to also deny the significance of these things.

81
00:09:54,584 --> 00:09:54,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

82
00:09:55,165 --> 00:09:58,326
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to accomplish freedom from suffering.

83
00:09:58,346 --> 00:10:04,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that denial extends to very subtle attitudes.

84
00:10:04,488 --> 00:10:09,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, people say, well, everything is just constantly changing.

85
00:10:09,069 --> 00:10:11,590
[SPEAKER_00]: None of this really exists.

86
00:10:12,350 --> 00:10:18,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, when they try to mix dhamma with science, like, it's just atoms and and the subatomic particles.

87
00:10:19,212 --> 00:10:28,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Or in behaving the way I do because of my genes or DNA, which I inherited from my parents, so we can take out the blame, which is sort of an inverted significance almost.

88
00:10:29,623 --> 00:10:34,868
[SPEAKER_03]: Or on top of the, like, oh, there's no mother, it's just a collection of add-ons.

89
00:10:37,642 --> 00:10:41,648
[SPEAKER_03]: And, I guess, go the other way, while simultaneously doing the exact same thing.

90
00:10:42,229 --> 00:10:50,241
[SPEAKER_03]: This might be for the maybe intense meditator who's like, oh, you know, it's not actually a mother, it's just a collection of like visual data.

91
00:10:52,875 --> 00:11:12,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, even if you don't talk with scientific terms, if you say, oh, no, no, that's just a perception, that's just a sight, and you might be, like, genuinely trying to practice the dhamma, like, because the Buddha says, see, everything is just, you know, in terms of the six sense bases of the five aggregates, but you're going the wrong way.

92
00:11:12,804 --> 00:11:20,268
[SPEAKER_00]: You're using it to basically, essentially, try to say, there is no mother and there's no father, because it's just a feeling, it's just a perception.

93
00:11:20,748 --> 00:11:21,869
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's on the wrong level.

94
00:11:21,949 --> 00:11:29,955
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to see, yes, it is just a feeling and just a perception that has a very great significance, like, compared to the other things.

95
00:11:30,235 --> 00:11:30,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

96
00:11:30,495 --> 00:11:45,686
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is that you need to be basically, uh, under, uh, acknowledging that things have a certain significance and then, within that basis, you, you know, develop the practice.

97
00:11:45,706 --> 00:11:45,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

98
00:11:45,947 --> 00:11:48,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, take a shortcut, basically.

99
00:11:48,428 --> 00:11:49,169
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to understand

100
00:11:56,473 --> 00:12:05,414
[SPEAKER_00]: You let it be what it is, and then you overcome your greed, your aversion, your delusion, in regard to the significance.

101
00:12:05,694 --> 00:12:09,615
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a subtle distinction that people wouldn't see.

102
00:12:10,435 --> 00:12:16,736
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's the same with, you know, acknowledging that there are ascetics and brahmins who realize this world and proclaim it.

103
00:12:17,276 --> 00:12:20,277
[SPEAKER_01]: That too is of significance, because if it wasn't like that,

104
00:12:20,997 --> 00:12:21,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, what are you even practicing?

105
00:12:22,898 --> 00:12:24,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Right?

106
00:12:24,198 --> 00:12:35,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to have that faith, like, yeah, there are people who see these things for themselves, and based on that actual knowledge that they have, they are capable of expounding that.

107
00:12:36,043 --> 00:12:36,964
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't have that,

108
00:12:38,332 --> 00:12:40,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, where's your practice?

109
00:12:40,293 --> 00:12:46,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, either you want to become a Buddha yourself, which is again overshooting the mark, because that's not quite necessary.

110
00:12:46,836 --> 00:12:49,417
[SPEAKER_01]: There already was a Buddha who has taught the dhamma.

111
00:12:50,038 --> 00:12:53,499
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the goal is not to become a Buddha, just become an Arhat.

112
00:12:53,519 --> 00:12:54,820
[SPEAKER_01]: That's good enough, basically.

113
00:12:56,141 --> 00:13:02,864
[SPEAKER_02]: And connected with that, there can also be a view, like, that the truth is sort of like

114
00:13:06,291 --> 00:13:09,053
[SPEAKER_00]: There are different truths, let's put it that way.

115
00:13:09,134 --> 00:13:17,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, when people try to mix all the different religions and say, you know, they're all talking about the same thing.

116
00:13:18,941 --> 00:13:20,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

117
00:13:21,082 --> 00:13:28,308
[SPEAKER_01]: I think this is sometimes called perennial philosophy, which is the overall term which says that basically all spiritual or religious traditions

118
00:13:29,108 --> 00:13:32,671
[SPEAKER_01]: They spring from the same source and they point to the same truth.

119
00:13:33,652 --> 00:13:36,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, for many people, that's pleasing to hold that view.

120
00:13:37,676 --> 00:13:38,417
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is that?

121
00:13:38,757 --> 00:13:41,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, in the end, it doesn't actually really matter what you do.

122
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:46,964
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as it's half religious or spiritual, for lack of better words, you can't go wrong.

123
00:13:47,485 --> 00:13:50,487
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that becomes a justification for

124
00:13:51,388 --> 00:13:59,495
[SPEAKER_01]: A massive lack of discernment, because we can just do this, that, and the other, and you think that if you just put it all on top of one another, it counts as progress.

125
00:13:59,635 --> 00:14:03,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you don't have to recognize the fact that, no, there is right and wrong.

126
00:14:04,099 --> 00:14:05,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there is.

127
00:14:05,240 --> 00:14:08,823
[SPEAKER_00]: There has to be some distinction between, like, not everything that you do

128
00:14:09,743 --> 00:14:12,744
[SPEAKER_00]: In the name of spirituality, it can't be considered right.

129
00:14:12,764 --> 00:14:22,146
[SPEAKER_00]: There are things that are taking you away from freedom, from greed, aversion and delusion, which is what the Buddha described as spirituality.

130
00:14:22,306 --> 00:14:22,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

131
00:14:22,786 --> 00:14:30,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just anything that you might regard as less mundane than the things that you're used to.

132
00:14:33,788 --> 00:14:35,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the other part.

133
00:14:35,788 --> 00:14:38,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to have a recognition that

134
00:14:41,070 --> 00:15:04,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just that there is a possibility to understand things, as it says that there are ascetics and brahmins who proclaim the world after having understood it, but that, eh... as the Buddha said in one of those suttas, he said, like, he came to his ascetics and asked them, oh, is there one culmination or are there many?

135
00:15:05,654 --> 00:15:07,174
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that's Majjhima Nikaya 11.

136
00:15:07,234 --> 00:15:08,715
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, like, eh...

137
00:15:11,336 --> 00:15:15,138
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, if they would answer rightly, they would say, there is one culmination.

138
00:15:15,758 --> 00:15:23,743
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he asked, is that culmination for one who is free from greed, aversion, delusion, or one who is not free from those things?

139
00:15:24,383 --> 00:15:30,086
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, basically, setting forth those criteria that

140
00:15:34,449 --> 00:15:41,542
[SPEAKER_00]: There has to be that distinction, basically, between... Not just anything that you would regard as liberation counts.

141
00:15:42,083 --> 00:15:44,708
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be, in terms of greed, aversion, delusion.

142
00:15:44,748 --> 00:15:46,491
[SPEAKER_00]: That would often not be emphasized.

143
00:15:47,411 --> 00:16:07,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Um, in terms of, like, when people talk about religious experiences and stuff, even in the context of Buddhism, um, you know, you might hear them talking about all sorts of other things, except, you know, how much, uh, concern for the world and, and, and, uh, desire and aversion has been destroyed.

144
00:16:07,876 --> 00:16:16,658
[SPEAKER_00]: More like, I had this, these magical insights and stuff that, that I didn't have before, but, you know, what, that, that part that is,

145
00:16:18,038 --> 00:16:23,560
[SPEAKER_00]: That is kind of the goalpost, or the thing that you should be aiming for, is not really being addressed.

146
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:29,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, that's another form of view that could obstruct the practice.

147
00:16:29,562 --> 00:16:33,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what is the freedom about, in the first place?

148
00:16:33,043 --> 00:16:34,103
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the goal about?

149
00:16:35,492 --> 00:16:52,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so people might actually, genuinely, on account of having some teaching or other, have an initial sense of relief, but that... but there needs to be a further discernment than that, because, you know, indulging in sensual pleasure can also give relief, but that is going in the wrong direction.

150
00:16:53,438 --> 00:16:59,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot just say, oh, I feel more at ease, or I feel a sense that the pressure has been... therefore it's moving in the right direction.

151
00:17:00,201 --> 00:17:02,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Or delighting in being, as the Buddha said, like there's a...

152
00:17:04,763 --> 00:17:05,443
[SPEAKER_00]: There's different things.

153
00:17:05,483 --> 00:17:17,789
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, there's ascetics who, like, even back in the day, they wouldn't be crudely engaging in sensuality, but they would be getting gratification out of their views and out of their practices.

154
00:17:18,609 --> 00:17:24,232
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as we discussed before, in the discussion about virtue and assurances.

155
00:17:24,272 --> 00:17:24,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

156
00:17:27,930 --> 00:17:35,456
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that comes within mundane right view, like having a direction of where you're actually going.

157
00:17:37,238 --> 00:17:41,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just anything that you start with, believing that that's what the dhamma is about.

158
00:17:42,022 --> 00:17:44,945
[SPEAKER_00]: You can take it for granted and start practicing with it.

159
00:17:46,186 --> 00:17:48,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So, essentially, that would be more of a

160
00:17:51,356 --> 00:17:57,460
[SPEAKER_00]: You would gain... like, 99% of the work is to question the assumptions that you're starting with.

161
00:17:57,781 --> 00:18:03,304
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not to just go with those assumptions and then try to build something on top of it, essentially.

162
00:18:03,705 --> 00:18:13,692
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what... but that's more pleasant because, like... Because one finds a full sense of safety and security on the level of use, precisely.

163
00:18:14,552 --> 00:18:19,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the right thing to do, so to say, in the practice.

164
00:18:21,277 --> 00:18:24,920
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that questioning of what... what are the views that I'm starting with?

165
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:35,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I... am I, you know, on some subtle level, not acknowledging the fact that there is mother and father, that there is action, that there are results of those actions?

166
00:18:36,228 --> 00:18:42,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I denying that, for example, even the the one we're talking about now, like,

167
00:18:45,077 --> 00:18:48,644
[SPEAKER_00]: That there are ascetics and brahmins who have realized this world and the next.

168
00:18:48,725 --> 00:18:54,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I've even heard some people saying that, you know, freedom from greed, aversion and delusion is not actually possible.

169
